{"ts": 0.091704, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\r\n", "len": 2}
{"ts": 0.091757, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;97mExport\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nA program the shell starts is a \u001b[1mchild\u001b[0m. It receives only the variables\r\n", "len": 100}
{"ts": 0.091772, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "you \u001b[1mexported\u001b[0m.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[92mpet=owl\u001b[0m                    \u001b[90m# set it again: every screen starts a fresh shell\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mbash -c 'echo pet=$pet'\u001b[0m    \u001b[90m# pet=    : the child sees nothing\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mexport pet\u001b[0m                 \u001b[90m# mark it for children\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mbash -c 'echo pet=$pet'\u001b[0m    \u001b[90m# pet=owl\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nThe single quotes matter: they leave \u001b[92m$pet\u001b[0m for the child to fill in.\r\n\r\n\u001b[93m▶ Run all four in order, then type \u001b[92mnext\u001b[93m.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 482}
{"ts": 0.095058, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;90mnext\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(go on)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mshow\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(this screen again)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mprev\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(back)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mquit\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(save and leave)\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 159}
